---
import MedicalNote from "../../components/ui/MedicalNote.astro";
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from "../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../lib/seo";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q: "What muscles do lying leg raises work?", a: "Lying leg raises primarily target the abdominal muscles, specifically the lower region of the rectus abdominis, alongside the hip flexors." },
  { q: "How do I perform lying leg raises correctly?", a: "Lie on a flat surface, maintain a neutral spine, and slowly lift your legs to a 90-degree angle. Lower them with control without arching your lower back." },
  { q: "Can lying leg raises help lose belly fat?", a: "While lying leg raises strengthen the core and build muscle, localized fat reduction is a myth. You need a caloric deficit to lose belly fat." },
  { q: "Why does my lower back hurt during leg raises?", a: "Lower back pain often results from arching the back due to weak core engagement. Ensure your lower back remains pressed against the floor throughout the movement." },
  { q: "Are lying leg raises suitable for beginners?", a: "Yes, but beginners should start with modified versions, such as bent-knee raises, to build foundational core strength before attempting straight-leg variations." },
  { q: "How often should I do lying leg raises?", a: "Incorporate them 2-3 times a week, ensuring adequate recovery time to prevent overtraining the abdominal and hip flexor muscles." }
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Lying Leg Raises — Proper Form, Muscles Worked, and E-E-A-T Guide"
  h1="Mastering Lying Leg Raises: A Comprehensive E-E-A-T Guide"
  description="Discover the definitive guide to lying leg raises. Learn proper form, muscles worked, common mistakes, and advanced core strengthening techniques based on scientific kinesiology."
  path="/workouts/lying-leg-raises/"
  category="Workouts"
  categoryPath="/workouts/"
  updated="September 2026"
  lead="Lying leg raises are a foundational bodyweight exercise that builds tremendous core stability, targeting the lower abdominal region and hip flexors. Master the biomechanics of this movement for optimal abdominal development."
  faqs={faqs}
>

  <MedicalNote />

  <div class="space-y-8 mt-8">
    <ArticleImage src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1571019614242-c5c5dee9f50b?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=1200" alt="Athlete executing core exercises with precision" class="h-[280px] w-full object-cover sm:h-[420px] rounded-xl" />

    <section class="card border-hairline p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft">
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-4">The Science Behind Lying Leg Raises</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The lying leg raise is highly esteemed in both athletic and rehabilitative domains due to its effectiveness in challenging the lumbo-pelvic hip complex. According to kinesiology principles, this exercise functions primarily as a closed-chain movement for the upper body and an open-chain movement for the lower extremities. 
      </p>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        As the legs elevate, the hip flexors act as prime movers, while the <em>rectus abdominis</em>, <em>transversus abdominis</em>, and internal/external obliques execute an isometric and eccentric contraction to stabilize the pelvis. This dynamic stabilization is essential for functional performance and preventing lower back pathology. Maintaining posterior pelvic tilt throughout the range of motion is the key to isolating the abdominal musculature and preventing lumbar hyperextension.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-4">Primary Muscular Targets (Kinesiological Breakdown)</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Understanding the muscular engagement is crucial for maximizing the hypertrophic and strength-building effects of the lying leg raise. 
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse border border-hairline rounded-xl">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-canvas-soft">
              <th class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-ink font-semibold">Muscle Group</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-ink font-semibold">Function During Exercise</th>
              <th class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-ink font-semibold">Role Type</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Rectus Abdominis</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Resists anterior pelvic tilt; stabilizes the spine</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Primary Stabilizer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Iliopsoas (Hip Flexors)</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Flexes the hip to lift the legs upward</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Prime Mover (Agonist)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Transversus Abdominis</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Increases intra-abdominal pressure; bracing</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Deep Stabilizer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Rectus Femoris</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Assists in hip flexion and maintains knee extension</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-hairline text-body">Synergist</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-4">Executing Proper Biomechanical Form</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Executing lying leg raises with impeccable form ensures you are targeting the core safely and effectively. Poor technique usually shifts the tension directly onto the lumbar spine, which can cause severe discomfort and potential discogenic issues. Here is the step-by-step procedure:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-3 text-body">
        <li><strong>Starting Position:</strong> Supine position on a firm mat. Extend the legs completely and keep them together. Position arms at your sides with palms facing down, or tuck hands slightly under your gluteal folds to assist with a posterior pelvic tilt.</li>
        <li><strong>Core Engagement:</strong> Perform a diaphragmatic brace. Exhale sharply to flatten your lower back against the floor, ensuring zero arching or gaps beneath your lumbar spine.</li>
        <li><strong>Concentric Phase (Upward Phase):</strong> Keeping the knees as straight as possible, contract your hip flexors and abdominals to lift the legs until your hips achieve approximately a 90-degree angle with your torso.</li>
        <li><strong>Eccentric Phase (Downward Phase):</strong> Slowly lower the legs over a duration of 3-4 seconds. Stop lowering the legs immediately if your lower back begins to arch off the floor. </li>
        <li><strong>Repetition Range:</strong> Strive for 3 to 4 sets of 10-15 controlled repetitions, focusing entirely on time under tension rather than sheer volume.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="card border-hairline p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-xl bg-canvas-soft">
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-4">Common Mistakes and Orthopedic Risk Factors</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Novices often compromise their form by neglecting the structural integrity of the pelvic position. The following errors should be strictly avoided to prevent injury and optimize neuromuscular adaptation:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-body">
        <li><strong>Excessive Lumbar Lordosis:</strong> Arching the lower back is the most pervasive error. It places shear forces on the L4-L5 and L5-S1 vertebrae, potentially leading to lower back pain. Always press the lumbar spine into the mat.</li>
        <li><strong>Using Momentum:</strong> Rapidly swinging the legs uses inertial force rather than muscle contraction, drastically reducing the exercise's hypertrophy stimulus. Focus on strict, tempo-based repetitions.</li>
        <li><strong>Neck Protraction:</strong> Straining the neck upward places unnecessary tension on the cervical spine. The head, neck, and upper trapezius must remain relaxed and in contact with the floor.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring the Eccentric Phase:</strong> Dropping the legs uncontrollably during the downward phase eliminates a significant portion of muscle fiber recruitment and significantly increases the risk of injury.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink mb-4">Progressions and Regressions</h2>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Depending on your current fitness level and core stability, you may need to regress the exercise or progress it to ensure progressive overload.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-lg text-ink font-semibold mt-4 mb-2">Regressions (For Beginners)</h3>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        If you find it difficult to maintain a flat lower back, start with <strong>Bent-Knee Raises</strong> or <strong>Single-Leg Raises</strong>. Shortening the lever arm significantly decreases the torque required by the core to maintain stability, allowing you to train the abdominals safely while building up to the full movement.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-lg text-ink font-semibold mt-4 mb-2">Progressions (For Advanced Trainees)</h3>
      <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Advanced athletes can increase the challenge by adding <strong>ankle weights</strong>, introducing an isometric hold at the bottom of the movement (hovering the feet 2 inches off the ground), or performing the exercise on a decline bench. Adding a <strong>Dragon Flag</strong> element or reverse crunch at the top of the raise will further recruit the entire abdominal wall, maximizing functional core power.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</ContentLayout>
